      "Registration with a provincial revenue authority typically requires the business to already hold a National Tax Number from FBR, along with basic documents such as CNIC copies, proof of business address, and bank account details. Once registered, the business receives a registration number specific to that authority and is expected to file periodic returns, usually monthly, declaring the value of services rendered and the tax charged to customers.",
      "Rates of sales tax on services vary by authority and by category of service, and they are revised from time to time through provincial finance acts, so businesses should not assume a rate remains constant year after year. Some services attract reduced rates, others are subject to fixed tax regimes, and certain services may be exempt altogether depending on the province. It is important to check the current schedule of rates published by the relevant authority before invoicing clients.",
      "Input tax adjustment rules also differ from the federal sales tax regime in some respects. A service provider registered with a provincial authority may be able to adjust input tax paid on certain purchases against its output tax liability, but the conditions for such adjustments, including invoice requirements and restrictions on specific categories of input tax, are set out in each authority's own rules. Keeping proper records of both sales and purchase invoices is essential to support any input tax claims during an audit.",
